          "severity": "warning",
          "detail": "Claim 4 states 'restoring broad NHS dental access would cost billions of pounds per year' but E14 quote specifically refers to 'restoring universal access' \u2014 the policy is more targeted (urgent/emergency care + check-ups for eligible groups only), so applying a universal-access cost estimate overstates the implied fiscal burden. The direction (worsens) survives because no funding source is stated, but the magnitude rationale leans on a figure not directly applicable to the policy as scoped."
        }
      ],
      "notes": "The verdict is broadly sound: the 'worsens / minor / low confidence' structure is appropriate given the absence of any stated funding mechanism and the documented real-terms decline in dental budgets. Tier discipline is maintained. The rationale correctly acknowledges the possibility of delivery within the existing \u00a34bn envelope and does not impute motive or party identity. The one concern is the application of Nuffield Trust's 'billions each year' estimate \u2014 derived from a universal-access scenario \u2014 to a more targeted policy; this warrants a warning but does not block, as the verdict's own text hedges appropriately and confidence is already set to low. No false balance, lever leakage, or magnitude inflation beyond the flagged warning."
